There are multiple methods for positional encoding in Transformers, including absolute positional encoding (ALiBi) and relative positional encoding. Absolute positional encoding assigns a unique embedding vector to each position in the input sequence to encode the position. This allows the model to understand the relative positions of tokens in the sequence, even when the order of the sequence is changed. Relative positional encoding, on the other hand, represents positional relationships between tokens without assigning absolute positions to them. ALiBi, or Absolutely-Located and -Invariant Bilinear Interactions, is a type of absolute positional encoding that uses a learnable feature matrix to generate positional embeddings. It was introduced in the paper "ALiBi: Absolutely-Located and Invariant Bilinear Interactions for Deep Learning Based Positional Encoding" by Liu et al. (2019). Overall, the choice of positional encoding method depends on the specific requirements of the task and the architecture of the model being used.
