[23/200] Running: How do attention heads capture contextual meaning?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Abstract. In the context of Deep Learning, attention is used to focus the network on relevant parts of the input sequence, enabling the model to build representations tailored to specific tasks, like sentence classification or machine translation. Recently, attention based models that use independent attention heads for all words in the input have achieved state-of-the-art performance in tasks like question answering and text classification. While there has been considerable effort to study the behavior of attention mechanisms in Transformer models at a high level, the question remains how those attention heads learn to reason about the input text with a focus on understanding context.

In this thesis we compare four attention head architectures: the Transformer, the Seq2seq (Luong Attention) model, the S2S with additive attention and the dot product attention model. Each model is tested on thealigning pairs corpus , a dataset that consists of pairs of sentences, where one sentence (“aligning sentence”) details aspects that some part of the other sentence (“target sentence”) focuses on, together with multiple question-answer pairs that are used to verify whether the aligning sentence accurately highlights the corresponding aspect in the target sentence. We compare the performance of each model on this task, using the F1-score of the predictions as a measure of quality.

In addition, we perform a qualitative analysis on the generated alignments to gain insights into which type of attention head best captures contextual meaning in the input text, by counting the occurrence of noun phrases, adjectival phrases, adverb phrases and other types of information in the generated alignments. As a result of this qualitative analysis we find that overall the dot product attention model performs worst, while the S2S with additive attention and the Transformer have the best performance, with the transformer performing better at capturing noun phrases and the S2S with additive attention favoring adjective phrases.

To allow for quantitative analysis of the models performances, we extract features describing the quality of the inputs, preprocess the alignments and questions, and re-run the models with and without the added features to test the effect of the features on the model’s quality.

We ultimately conclude that advances in the modeling of attention functions on the input text play an important role in the models ability to capture context and help build more accurate models.
