A) For accurate throughput measurement while dealing with variable prompts, you can use a combination of `iperf3` and `netcat` (or `nc`) command. Here's a step-by-step guide for you:

1. Install iperf3:
	```
	sudo apt-get install iperf3
```

2. Start the server using iperf3:

	Make sure your client and server are connected and you have the IP address of the server. In this example, we assume the client IP is `192.168.1.2` and the server IP is `192.168.1.3`.

	```
	server$ iperf3 -s --logfile iperf3_log_server
```

3. Start the client using netcat or nc:

	```
	client$ nc -n 192.168.1.3 5201 | iperf3 - clinical_throughput -c 192.168.1.3 -w 60 -t 10
```

In the above command, replace `clinical_throughput` with the protocol name you are testing for (e.g., `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp`, etc.)

`nc` reads the data from the client and forwards it to the iperf3 server which takes care of timing and measuring the throughput. By using `-w 60 -t 10`, iperf3 performs the test for 60 seconds and stops after 10 seconds to ensure you have proper data for the calculation.

Once the test is complete, you can view the throughput results from the server log (iperf3_log_server) and analyze them as needed.
[136/200] Running: How does `max_new_tokens` differ from `max_length`?...

