[141/200] Running: What are model shards and when are they used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Model shards are a feature specific to large-scale models, which allow for faster response times and reduced resource usage when training or serving a model.

Large-scale models can become problematic as they grow larger in terms of parameters (and/or the size of the inputs they take in), because the memory and computational requirements to train or serve these models increase significantly. To help address this, model sharding separates a large model into smaller segments, or shards, which can be processed and executed independently to reduce the overall resource requirements.

Model sharding has several use cases:

1. Accelerated Training: By distributed training a large-scale model across multiple workers or machines using sharded model weights, you can train large models more quickly and with less memory per worker or machine.
2. Reduced Memory Usage: Sharding a model allows each worker or machine to load a smaller portion of the model, which can result in significant memory savings, especially on devices with limited memory.
3. Parallel Serving: During inference or serving, multiple requests can be processed simultaneously by distributing the sharded model segments across multiple worker or machine instances. This parallelism can improve response times and scale the model's handling capacity beyond a single worker or machine.

By applying model sharding techniques, you can make it feasible to train and serve larger models on a broader range of devices, from on-device mobile devices to cloud-based servers.
