[158/200] Running: What is a quantized model and how is it served?...

🧠 LLM Response:

End-to-End Quantization (E2EQ) is a process within the machine learning (ML) pipeline that reduces the computational and memory footprint of a trained ML model to run it efficiently on specialized hardware that requires less power and costs less. Quantization can be applied to the entire model (end-to-end) or to specific parts (inference-time or training-time quantization).

An E2EQ model goes through a three-step workflow:
1. Model selection or model-specific quantization: in this step, model architecture is kept unchanged, but the choice of quantization coefficients and their precision are optimized.
2. Model conversion: in this step, the weights, activations, and/or opcode (operations) of the ML model are converted to the desired quantization format that can work with the target hardware.
3. Fine-tuning: in this step, if the quantized model experiences degradation in performance during inference, the model is fine-tuned to give it the pre-quantization performance level.

Serving a quantized model:

Typically, quantized models are served using model servers or pre-packaged solutions. Open-source tools like TensorFlow Serving, ONNX, TFLite, and TensorFlow Lite Model Maker can be used to deploy quantized models. These tools facilitate serving quantized models on a variety of hardware platforms using APIs.
