Let's expand our usage to something a bit more fancy, to demonstrate something useful to our purposing.

## Consuming Large Streams

When working with HTTP via `curl`, there's a limit to the size of data you transfer using a single request. By default, that limit is 1 megabyte (MB), so that's a limitation we should be aware of.

To overcome this limitation, you can break the whole file into chunks (e.g. 1024 bytes) and process individual data pieces in memory, such that you don't have to store the entire clump of data in memory. That's how the computer is doing this in the first place and operating within its limitations!

To make this work, let's make use of an environment variable of `CURLOPT_INFOFUNCTION` that, in short, allows us to make use of a callback rather than promptly having `curl` dump the contents in the terminal or elsewhere. In doing so create and pass an own callback to fetch, line by line, the JSON data, which we'll further process with our trusty `jq` power-tool.

